            "name": "Overview",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-YrtWd0RzLoNr3lxIrLXlZOeMgqe\">Overall, while milk production in the Netherlands remained relatively stable and Australia experienced a decline, both Tanzania and Guatemala showed increasing trends during the period. Additionally, the Netherlands and Australia produced significantly more milk than the other two nations, with their output being over 100 times greater.</div>\n</div>",
            "question_id": 13786,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 397,
            "sort": 3,
            "name": "Body 1",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-HHqCdrOZSo25PMxuHxileyAxggc\">Looking at the major producers, in 1990, the Netherlands and Australia had similar production levels of approximately 11.2 million litres each. The Netherlands maintained steady production throughout the period, with only minor fluctuations, ending at about 11.5 million litres in 2010. In contrast, Australia's output declined notably, falling by roughly 2 million litres to reach 9.2 million litres by the end of the period.</div>\n</div>",
